def section(text,start,end,new):
 a=text.index(start);b=text.index(end,a);return text[:a]+new.strip()+'\n\n'+text[b:]
s=s.replace(old.splitlines()[0],'# Climate information and temporal reconciliation of subseasonal rainfall forecasts in eight Australian domains',1)
abstract='''Climate information may improve rainfall forecasts at individual weeks or longer accumulation periods, but these gains need to be distinguished from the value of combining the scales. We examine this distinction using ECMWF hindcasts for eight Australian domains, nested year validation, an ACCESS-S2 comparison and a previously unused ECMWF forecast period. Marginal calibration uses rainfall predictors alone, adds climate indices to weekly or aggregate forecasts, or adds them at every node. Reconciliation enforces nonnegative, consistent accumulations. In retrospective ECMWF validation, aggregate climate calibration with reconciliation lowers the continuous ranked probability score relative to climate-informed weekly summation by 1.05% of raw forecast score (95% interval 0.61–1.50%). ACCESS-S2 supports the same comparison, although the incremental climate effect within reconciliation remains uncertain. Adjusting every node worsens ECMWF scores, while weekly-only and aggregate-only climate adjustments perform similarly when both are reconciled. In the frozen 2020 test, six domains favour aggregate reconciliation, but the pooled interval includes zero and the confirmation criterion is not met. Reanalysis shows that humid summer westerlies accompany the northern rainfall season, without establishing a reliable circulation rule for forecast improvement. Direct aggregate forecasts can add value, but its attribution and transfer depend on the information configuration and evaluation period.'''
s=section(s,'## Abstract','Keywords:','## Abstract\n\n'+abstract)
s=section(s,'The unresolved issue examined here','## 2 Data and experimental design','''The unresolved issue is how much a forecast gains from climate information, and how much it gains from using direct aggregate predictions alongside the weeks. These changes are often made together. Recent subseasonal postprocessing can improve ensemble probabilities (Guan et al. 2026), but that does not establish which accumulation scales should receive additional predictors. We hold the rainfall calibration family fixed and compare climate information at weekly, aggregate and all hierarchy nodes. Summation and reconciliation then provide distinct ways of constructing the longer accumulations.

The study asks whether direct aggregates improve on climate-informed weekly summation, and whether extending climate adjustments to every scale adds further value. Eight-domain and second-system analyses examine the consistency of the answer. An untouched issued-forecast period tests temporal transfer, while independent reanalysis fields describe the northern monsoon environment in which the original signal was strongest. The original factorial comparisons retain their primary status. The geographical expansion and additional controls were motivated by those results; the 2020 test was specified before its forecast scores were calculated. This sequence separates evidence used to develop the questions from evidence used to test their transfer.''')
s=s.replace('giving 228 origins in the N domain.','giving 228 origins in each of the eight domains.').replace('average the forecast cells within N.','average the forecast cells within each domain.')
anchor='### 2.3 Climate predictors'
s=s.replace(anchor,'''A separate test uses all 53 Thursday ECMWF operational forecast origins in 2020. Before their scores were inspected, the protocol fixed members 0–10 from the 51-member archive to match the hindcast size, all eight domains and the existing calibration settings. Training retains only 2000–2019 origins whose targets end by 31 December 2019. AGCD through 28 January 2021 completes the final forecast. All forecasts and parameter files were saved before scoring. The packing audit repeats the original-GRIB comparison at four endpoints for the control member of all 53 files. This is retrospective transfer to previously unused issued forecasts: the training archive pools configurations produced during 2020, and the index archives include revisions, so the experiment does not reconstruct the information available to a forecaster on every historical issue date.

'''+anchor)
s=s.replace('### 2.4 Validation and experiment sequence','''Monthly NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis 1 winds and specific humidity at 850 hPa provide a separate circulation diagnostic (Kalnay et al. 1996). The IRI Data Library supplies 2.5° fields for 2000–2019 over 100–155°E and 25°S–5°N. Its monthly coordinate is mapped by calendar-month identity; the 360-day indexing convention is not interpreted as daily verification time. The fixed monsoon diagnostic is area-mean zonal wind over 120–140°E and 15–5°S. Summer and winter composites describe the seasonal background. Calendar-year December–February means are paired with northern forecast-score differences to test whether that background also explains year-to-year gains. These fields are used for interpretation, not as new forecast predictors.

### 2.4 Validation and experiment sequence''')
s=s.replace('H1 therefore tests a defined climate adjustment within the existing forecast model.','''H1 therefore tests a defined climate adjustment within the existing forecast model.

Two further configurations complete the placement comparison. Hweekly applies the same adjustment only to the four weeks, retains rainfall-only direct aggregates and reconciles them. H2 adjusts all seven nodes and reconciles them. Both reuse the corresponding fitted weekly and aggregate marginals, while covariance is re-estimated from their own inner errors. BU-clim sums the climate-adjusted weeks without direct aggregates. Aggregate-only climate information has no effect on bottom-up forecasts because that construction discards aggregate nodes; weekly-only and all-node summation both equal BU-clim. Supplementary Table S22 gives the distinct configurations and avoids counting duplicate baselines.''')
s=section(s,'### 3.6 Coherent mixtures and forecast selection','## 4 Results','''### 3.6 Extension inference and the frozen forecast test

Eight-domain ECMWF effects use equal domain weights after dividing annual paired differences by each domain's full-record raw CRPS. The 50000 bootstrap draws resample common years across domains, preserving their spatial dependence. Annual sign flips and Holm adjustment cover four exploratory comparisons of H1 with raw, BU-rain, BU-clim and H0. This post hoc synthesis does not replace the original three-test family. The Hweekly and H2 contrasts have descriptive intervals. ACCESS-S2 uses nineteen common year blocks and a separate Holm family for H1 versus H0, H1 versus BU-clim, and H0 versus BU-rain (Supplementary Tables S23 and S24).

The protocol dated 25 October 2026 fixes one confirmation contrast for the new 2020 forecast period: H1 minus BU-clim 28-day CRPS, averaged across the eight domains after normalisation by each domain's raw training score. A circular moving-block bootstrap uses four consecutive weekly origins, 50000 draws and identical indices across domains. The success criterion is a negative mean and a two-sided 95% interval with its upper bound below zero. Six- and eight-origin blocks assess sensitivity. These intervals describe within-year sampling under the block approximation; a single year cannot estimate interannual uncertainty. Other forecasts are retained as descriptive comparators. Supplementary Table S25 reports all scores and the locked protocol.

For the circulation diagnostic, a paired bootstrap over twenty calendar years gives an interval for the Spearman association between summer monsoon flow and H1-minus-H0 CRPS. Summer combines January, February and December origins within each calendar year, matching the forecast-year grouping. The diagnostic was fixed after the northern seasonal result was known and is exploratory. Monthly wind and humidity composites describe large-scale background conditions; they do not resolve individual MJO events or establish a causal DMI pathway (Supplementary Table S26).''')
s=s.replace('Twenty additional full year-block permutations','Ninety-nine full year-block permutations')
s=s.replace('Supplementary Tables S14–S18 retain all extension diagnostics.','''Supplementary Tables S14–S18 retain the original extension diagnostics. Separate probability-mixture and forecast-selection experiments examine the marginal value of added complexity; their complete methods, results and decision scores are in Supplementary Notes 3 and 4, Tables S19–S21 and Fig. S6.''')
s=s.replace('Across every possible five-year omission, H1 minus BU-rain remains negative, but its size varies substantially (Supplementary Table S14). These omissions do not refit the models.','''Across all 15504 five-year omissions, H1 minus BU-rain ranges from −0.884 to −0.094 mm, with median −0.543 mm and a central 95% omission range of −0.767 to −0.301 mm. Against H0, the median is −0.375 mm, the central range is −0.581 to −0.149 mm, and the full range reaches +0.039 mm. Three omissions reverse that contrast. These are distributions of retained score means, not sampling confidence intervals or refitted forecasts (Supplementary Table S14; Fig. S8).''')
s=s.replace('The interval does not establish an additional reconciliation advantage.','This individual-domain interval includes zero; the eight-domain synthesis is reported below.')
s=s.replace('In the twenty full year-block permutations, CRPS ranges from 20.827 to 21.393 mm; none is below the unpermuted 20.546 mm, giving a one-sided Monte Carlo p value of 0.048.','In the 99 full year-block permutations, CRPS ranges from 20.441 to 21.444 mm. Two scores are below the unpermuted 20.546 mm, giving a one-sided Monte Carlo p value of 0.030.')
s=s.replace('The annual scores are shown in Fig. 8 and Supplementary Table S12.','The annual scores are shown in Supplementary Fig. S7 and Table S12.')
a=s.index('### 4.9 Transfer across geographical domains and forecast systems');b=s.index('## 5 Discussion',a)
s=s[:a]+'''### 4.9 Eight-domain comparisons clarify the information contrast

The four additional ECMWF domains extend the comparison without selecting locations by forecast performance. H1 has lower mean CRPS than BU-clim in all eight domains (Fig. 5). The domain-averaged difference is −1.046% of raw CRPS (95% interval −1.501 to −0.606%; Holm-adjusted p = 0.00084). Against H0, however, the difference is −0.331% (−0.742 to 0.056%; adjusted p = 0.130). Thus the coherent aggregate forecast outperforms weekly climate summation in this retrospective sample, while the incremental climate adjustment within reconciliation remains uncertain (Table 6; Supplementary Table S23).

The completed placement comparison changes the attribution. Hweekly minus H1 is +0.025% of raw CRPS (−0.212 to 0.260%), with four domains favourable to each configuration. H2 is worse than H1 in every domain, averaging +0.978% (0.466–1.441%). Hweekly also improves on BU-clim, whereas H2 minus BU-clim has an interval spanning zero (Supplementary Table S22). The gain over weekly summation therefore cannot be assigned solely to placing climate information at the aggregates. Both configurations that retain some rainfall-only marginals perform similarly, while adjusting every node adds flexibility without improving this comparison.

Within N, the four constituent forecast cells also differ: H1 minus BU-rain ranges from −1.259 mm in the northwestern cell to +0.044 mm in the southeastern cell (Supplementary Table S13). The boxes and their cells are correlated spatial supports, not independent samples of continental performance.

### 4.10 The second system supports one contrast but leaves another uncertain

Across eight ACCESS-S2 domains, H1 minus BU-clim is −5.726% of raw CRPS (95% interval −7.659 to −3.900%; Holm-adjusted p = 0.00009), with favourable point estimates in every domain. H1 minus H0 is instead +0.468% (−0.556 to 1.451%; adjusted p = 0.440). The second system therefore supports the comparison with weekly climate summation but provides insufficient evidence for an incremental aggregate climate benefit (Table 6; Supplementary Table S24).

Northern H0 improves on BU-rain by −0.759 mm (−1.313 to −0.252 mm). H1 adds only −0.025 mm relative to H0, with an interval from −1.166 to 1.431 mm that includes both zero and the ECMWF northern difference. The original summer H1-minus-H0 interval is also broad (Supplementary Table S17). Pooling H0 minus BU-rain across all domains gives an interval spanning zero. These results distinguish a local reconciliation gain from a climate-information gain; they do not establish equivalence between systems.

### 4.11 The frozen 2020 test does not meet its confirmation criterion

In the previously unused issued-forecast period, H1 has lower CRPS than BU-clim in six of eight domains. The normalised pooled difference is −0.923%, with a four-origin block interval of −2.555 to 0.413%. Six- and eight-origin blocks also include zero. The predeclared confirmation criterion is therefore not met (Table 6; Fig. 8).

Northern H1 scores 14.672 mm, compared with 14.980 mm for BU-clim and 17.144 mm for raw ECMWF. The eastern coastal and southwestern domains favour BU-clim over H1. These domain scores remain descriptive because the fixed test concerned the pooled contrast. H2 improves on H1 in two domains and worsens it in six. All seven forecast configurations and every domain are retained in Supplementary Table S25. This new year is consistent with a favourable average direction but does not establish that the retrospective advantage transfers reliably between years.

### 4.12 Monsoon circulation explains the seasonal setting

The independent reanalysis identifies a clear seasonal reversal in the fixed northern circulation box (Fig. 10). Mean 850 hPa zonal wind changes from +3.04 m s⁻¹ in summer to −6.81 m s⁻¹ in winter, while specific humidity falls from 11.18 to 8.43 g kg⁻¹. Northern-domain AGCD rainfall averages 247.71 mm per month in summer and 1.04 mm in winter. The humid summer westerlies provide a physical setting for the concentration of rainfall and millimetre-valued forecast losses in the wet season.

Across the twenty summer-year pairs, the Spearman correlation between zonal wind and H1-minus-H0 CRPS is −0.25, with a year-bootstrap interval of −0.66 to 0.23. Stronger summer westerlies are therefore not a reliable discriminator of forecast improvement in this sample. The circulation fields explain the season in which large gains or losses can occur, but do not identify the climate index responsible for a gain or justify conditioning forecast choice on this wind diagnostic alone.

'''+s[b:]
s=section(s,'## 5 Discussion','## 6 Conclusions','''## 5 Discussion

The main distinction is between improving a coherent forecast and identifying why it improves. H1 outperforms BU-clim across the eight ECMWF domains in retrospective validation, and ACCESS-S2 supports that comparison. This is evidence that direct aggregate forecasts can contribute beyond climate-informed weekly summation. It is not, by itself, evidence that climate predictors work better at aggregate scales. Once weekly climate forecasts are also reconciled with rainfall-only aggregates, their performance is close to H1. The configuration that adjusts every node is worse in the ECMWF archive. Table 7 summarises these conclusions and their evidential scope.

One interpretation is that rainfall-only marginals provide useful constraints when climate adjustments are uncertain. Reconciliation combines forecasts whose estimation errors and climate responses differ; applying the same predictor family at every scale may reduce that complementarity or propagate a shared calibration error. The experiment shows the score pattern, but it does not identify which of these mechanisms dominates. The small differences from adding fortnightly nodes also show that more aggregation equations alone do not supply useful information.

The northern seasonal setting is physically coherent with Australian rainfall research. MJO-related changes in convection, rainfall occurrence and persistence operate within a wet monsoon background (Wheeler et al. 2009; Cowan et al. 2024). Reanalysis confirms humid low-level summer westerlies and a dry winter regime. This explains why wet-season forecasts dominate scores in millimetres, while the uncertain year-to-year association with wind strength prevents a stronger mechanism claim. The DMI-only model was inspected after the full model; its favourable point estimate does not identify an independent IOD pathway. IOD events generally peak before summer and weaken with the monsoon (Australian Bureau of Meteorology, undated b), so the lagged DMI may retain information about earlier or correlated ocean conditions. Resolving that possibility requires a separate event-scale analysis.

Temporal transfer is more demanding than retrospective agreement. In the northern 2015–2019 forward period, BU-clim outperforms H1 and raw ECMWF outperforms both. In the untouched 2020 issued-forecast period, the pooled contrast returns to a favourable direction, but its interval crosses zero. The prespecified confirmation condition fails. Together with the five-year omission distribution, these results show that mean gains can depend on the training and verification climates. A new forecast year adds an independent evaluation period, but cannot establish interannual stability.

Minimum-trace theory concerns linear unbiased forecasts under a specified covariance. Here covariance is estimated, samples are constrained to be nonnegative and scores are evaluated out of sample. The optimistic covariance diagnostic applies to this implementation and archive. It does not show that all covariance-based reconciliation is systematically biased. Likewise, results for specific aggregated autoregressive models (Neubauer and Filzmoser 2024) do not determine how these rainfall distributions behave.

Dependence reconstruction also matters. Historical ranks broaden the northern summed distribution, while matched ECC retains the local H1-minus-H0 direction with different absolute calibration. Statistical calibration and cross-scale dependence therefore need separate checks (Bellier et al. 2023). The supplementary power-map audit illustrates the consequence of using different fitting and application populations. The supplementary mixture comparison addresses a related complexity question: changing weights with the tested forecast-state variables does not reliably improve on a fixed mixture. This is a result about the marginal value of that specification, not a general limit on adaptive forecasting.

The geographical scope is eight fixed boxes and the sampled forecast systems. These results do not establish effectiveness across all Australia, and they do not support the conclusion that reconciliation is useless. They support a conditional comparison: direct aggregates improve on climate-informed weekly summation in the retrospective archives, while information placement within reconciliation is less decisive and the new-year test remains inconclusive. ACCESS-S2 has fewer and differently spaced origins than ECMWF, and shares its historical climate years; it provides a second-system check rather than wholly independent climate replication.

For forecast practice, calibration should first be compared with the raw ensemble. A climate-informed weekly sum then supplies a coherent reference for evaluating direct aggregate information. Keeping rainfall-only alternatives exposes whether added predictors improve the forecast or merely make every marginal more flexible. Evidence from held-out scores, interval calibration and event probabilities should determine which configuration is retained. Australian studies of SAM–rainfall relationships and moisture transport offer the same practical motivation for testing predictors in their intended season and region (Firth et al. 2024; Reid et al. 2024).''')
s=section(s,'## 6 Conclusions','## Data availability','''## 6 Conclusions

Direct aggregate forecasts add value relative to climate-informed weekly summation in the eight-domain ECMWF cross-validation and ACCESS-S2 comparisons. The result corrects an overly broad conclusion of no reconciliation benefit. It also requires careful attribution: within reconciliation, weekly-only and aggregate-only climate adjustments have similar average skill, while applying climate adjustments at all nodes worsens the ECMWF comparison. The original tests of an incremental aggregate climate effect remain inconclusive.

Temporal validation qualifies this retrospective advantage. The northern 2015–2019 forward comparison favours weekly climate summation, and the fixed eight-domain 2020 test does not meet its confirmation criterion. The new year supplies independent forecast-period evidence with a favourable mean direction, but insufficient precision to establish stable transfer. A single year cannot resolve interannual generalisation.

Independent circulation data explain the wet-season setting through humid low-level westerlies, while their uncertain association with annual score gains leaves the predictive mechanism unresolved. The combined evidence supports evaluating direct aggregates against coherent weekly forecasts and retaining rainfall-only controls. More nodes, more climate adjustments and more flexible combination weights each require their own evidence of added skill. Temporal consistency makes forecasts agree across accumulation periods; its predictive benefit depends on the forecasts and climate conditions being combined.''')
